  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "vHXixnzYnBP6w4D7oaV/jpSVrbc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:44:57.888Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`repository.properties` ships `audit.enabled=true` alongside `audit.alfresco-access.enabled=false`. The first property is a decoy: it arms the auditing framework without producing any records. Alfresco's own documentation says so directly \u2014 *\"enabling this property does not necessarily result in the generation of audit data\"* \u2014 and elsewhere, *\"The auditing system is disabled by default, as it has the potential to impact performance.\"*\n\nThe consequence is that a default Alfresco Community install has no content audit trail whatsoever. DoD 5015 auditing (`audit.dod5015.enabled=false`) and download auditing are off for the same reason.\n\nThis matters more than a missing feature would. An organisation that believes it has an audit trail because the config says `audit.enabled=true` has a governance gap it cannot see, and will discover it at the moment it needs the record.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-20T08:44:57.888430Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-20T08:44:57.888434Z"
    }
  },
